  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front will cross through the area into \ntonight, bringing the potential for a round of showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nAnother cold front approaches into tonight. Highest instability\nwill be across Delmarva and southern New Jersey where SBCAPE \nvalues will be as high as 500 to 750 J/kg, and MUCAPE values \nwill be slightly higher. PWATs will be 1.5 to 1.75 inches in \nDelmarva where locally heavy rain is possible. In these areas \nwill be the highest chances for convection, mainly after\nmidnight through around daybreak Friday.\n\nFollowing any morning showers, Friday will shape up to be a nice\nday with clouds breaking later in the day and humidity falling\ninto the afternoon. Highs in the upper 70s to low 80s in most \nareas.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another disturbance will approach the area \nSaturday into Sunday and produce a round of showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nIn the wake of Thursday's cold frontal passage, high pressure \nwill briefly build into the region for Friday. Dry conditions \nare expected with temperatures generally in the 70s to near 80. \nHowever, guidance continues to indicate that the front will \nstall somewhere over the Carolinas before lifting slowly back \nnorth towards the area for the weekend. A mid-level shortwave \nlooks to pass through the area Saturday night and an area of \nsurface low pressure is likely to ride along the boundary as it \nshifts slowly northward into the area. \n \nConfidence is increasing in scattered shower and storm activity\nacross the region Saturday through Saturday night. As of right \nnow, instability looks to be meager, limiting any severe storm \nthreat. However, forecast soundings across the area indicate \ndeeply saturated vertical profiles, especially across the \nsouthern half or so of the area. PWATs look to exceed 2 inches. \nGiven this, any storms across the area could be efficient \nrainfall producers. As such, there does appear to be some \npotential for isolated instances of flash flooding. The WPC has \nhighlighted the entire area with a Marginal Risk (1 of 4) in \ntheir latest Excessive Rainfall Outlook to account for this \npotential. \n\nSome time late Saturday night or Sunday, the front and low \npressure will track offshore, bringing an end to the shower and \nstorm chances and a return of tranquil conditions to start the \nnext work week. With that said, timing discrepancies limit \nconfidence on Sunday, as a slower progressing system could lead \nto rain chances lingering at least into Sunday afternoon. \n\nIn terms of temperatures, they look to hold fairly steady in \nthe mid 70s to mid 80s through the weekend and into early next \nweek, with perhaps a slow warming trend.",
